1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,167
For a whole year, Mrs.

2
00:00:02,167 --> 00:00:04,894
Rao bought a Laddoo Lab 6-pack every week.

3
00:00:05,194 --> 00:00:07,625
Then, in July, she quietly stopped.

4
00:00:08,175 --> 00:00:10,140
Nobody noticed for a month.

5
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:13,644
Laddoo Lab had lost its
very first regular customer.

6
00:00:14,294 --> 00:00:15,194
Mrs.

7
00:00:14,789 --> 00:00:16,787
Rao is a busy working mother.

8
00:00:16,787 --> 00:00:20,714
Every school morning, a laddoo
goes into her daughter's tiffin.

9
00:00:21,064 --> 00:00:26,007
By now, Laddoo Lab has hundreds of
customers like her, in shops and online.

10
00:00:26,357 --> 00:00:30,688
So Zoya asks: how do we remember
each one, and keep them coming back?

11
00:00:31,338 --> 00:00:35,356
The answer is customer
relationship management, or CRM.

12
00:00:35,656 --> 00:00:40,967
Kotler and Armstrong define it as building
and maintaining profitable customer

13
00:00:40,967 --> 00:00:42,015
relationships,

14
00:00:42,365 --> 00:00:46,125
by delivering superior customer
value and satisfaction.

15
00:00:46,475 --> 00:00:50,264
In simple words: know each customer,
serve them well, and keep them.

16
00:00:50,614 --> 00:00:51,516
Why bother?

17
00:00:51,516 --> 00:00:55,871
Winning a new customer can cost five
to ten times more than keeping one

18
00:00:55,871 --> 00:00:57,127
you already have.

19
00:00:57,777 --> 00:01:00,045
Let's watch CRM work for Mrs.

20
00:01:00,045 --> 00:01:01,783
Rao, in four steps.

21
00:01:02,133 --> 00:01:03,033
Step one.

22
00:01:02,857 --> 00:01:03,757
Know her.

23
00:01:03,581 --> 00:01:05,215
Dev keeps a customer book.

24
00:01:05,215 --> 00:01:08,120
Her name, her flavour,
and the day she orders.

25
00:01:08,470 --> 00:01:09,370
Mrs.

26
00:01:09,010 --> 00:01:12,703
Rao likes ragi jaggery, and
she orders every Sunday.

27
00:01:13,053 --> 00:01:13,953
Step two.

28
00:01:13,865 --> 00:01:15,331
Serve her personally.

29
00:01:15,331 --> 00:01:17,660
Every Saturday, one short message.

30
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:19,751
Your tiffin pack for the week?

31
00:01:20,101 --> 00:01:21,037
Step three.

32
00:01:21,037 --> 00:01:22,758
Watch for warning signs.

33
00:01:22,758 --> 00:01:25,791
In July, the book shows
two missed Sundays.

34
00:01:26,141 --> 00:01:27,041
Step four.

35
00:01:27,029 --> 00:01:28,130
Win her back.

36
00:01:28,130 --> 00:01:30,639
Zoya calls her, and mostly listens.

37
00:01:30,989 --> 00:01:33,862
Her daughter is bored of the same flavour.

38
00:01:33,862 --> 00:01:37,110
So Zoya sends a free
coconut jowar pack to try.

39
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:38,663
By August, Mrs.

40
00:01:38,663 --> 00:01:40,591
Rao orders every Sunday again.

41
00:01:40,591 --> 00:01:43,449
And she tells two friends
at the school gate.

42
00:01:44,099 --> 00:01:45,642
A sale happens once.

43
00:01:45,642 --> 00:01:48,086
A relationship pays back for years.

44
00:01:48,786 --> 00:01:51,338
CRM starts with knowing the customer.

45
00:01:51,338 --> 00:01:55,645
Then, Kotler and Armstrong describe
three ways to build loyalty.

46
00:01:55,995 --> 00:01:57,295
Know the customer.

47
00:01:57,295 --> 00:02:01,752
A pharmacy remembers your regular
medicines, and reminds you to refill.

48
00:02:02,102 --> 00:02:03,535
Financial benefits.

49
00:02:03,535 --> 00:02:05,315
Reward people who stay.

50
00:02:05,315 --> 00:02:08,767
An airline gives free
flights to frequent flyers.

51
00:02:09,117 --> 00:02:10,320
Social benefits.

52
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:11,637
Make it personal.

53
00:02:11,637 --> 00:02:15,701
A bank gives its big customers
their own relationship manager.

54
00:02:16,051 --> 00:02:17,279
Structural ties.

55
00:02:17,279 --> 00:02:18,675
Make staying easy.

56
00:02:18,675 --> 00:02:23,199
A food delivery app saves your
address, so ordering takes seconds.

57
00:02:23,849 --> 00:02:25,942
But CRM can go too far.

58
00:02:26,292 --> 00:02:27,436
The good side.

59
00:02:27,436 --> 00:02:31,427
Customers feel known, and loyal
customers bring steady sales.

60
00:02:31,777 --> 00:02:32,951
The hard side.

61
00:02:32,951 --> 00:02:37,543
Too many messages feel like spam,
and customer data can be misused.

62
00:02:37,893 --> 00:02:42,189
Dev wants to message everyone every
day, and share the list with a gym

63
00:02:42,189 --> 00:02:43,089
nearby.

64
00:02:43,171 --> 00:02:44,218
Zoya says no.

65
00:02:44,218 --> 00:02:47,467
One message a week, only
to people who said yes.

66
00:02:47,467 --> 00:02:49,310
And the list stays private.

67
00:02:49,660 --> 00:02:55,161
In India, the Digital Personal Data
Protection Act of 2023 says firms need

68
00:02:55,161 --> 00:02:58,701
your clear consent to
use your personal data.

69
00:02:59,051 --> 00:02:59,951
The lesson?

70
00:02:59,887 --> 00:03:03,484
Use what customers share to
serve them, never to spam them.

71
00:03:04,134 --> 00:03:05,068
Quick check.

72
00:03:05,068 --> 00:03:06,802
Laddoo Lab sees that Mrs.

73
00:03:06,802 --> 00:03:10,029
Rao missed two weeks, and
calls to win her back.

74
00:03:10,029 --> 00:03:11,069
What is this?

75
00:03:12,969 --> 00:03:14,879
Customer relationship management!

76
00:03:14,879 --> 00:03:18,184
They know her, spot the
warning sign, and keep her.

77
00:03:18,784 --> 00:03:19,797
So, remember.

78
00:03:20,097 --> 00:03:24,036
CRM means: know each customer,
serve them well, and keep them.

79
00:03:24,386 --> 00:03:27,726
Keeping a customer costs far
less than winning a new one.

80
00:03:28,076 --> 00:03:32,672
Build loyalty with rewards, a
personal touch, and easy ways to stay.

81
00:03:33,272 --> 00:03:35,728
Next time, we'll work out what Mrs.

82
00:03:35,728 --> 00:03:39,605
Rao is really worth to Laddoo
Lab: customer lifetime value.
